[gd_resource type="Environment" load_steps=2 format=3 uid="uid://cq2gubrangeenv"]

; Environment for Highsun Grounds. It was the Hollow's own night (D-113), then
; the hour before it, and it is now the middle of the morning — the owner asked
; for the sun higher in the air and for the name off the glowworms, and this
; file is the second half of that.
;
; The night was defensible, it was measured, and it lost. The argument for it
; was that the range is a corner of the Hollow's world and a different sky over
; it would say it was somewhere else. The argument against it is what a range is
; *for*: you go there to read a target at forty-five metres and to see where
; your spear landed, and at 11 degrees of moon the peat, the timber and the
; distance posts were one dark mass with lantern heads floating in it. The fix
; at the time was to push the fill until the ground existed — moon 1.15, sky
; ambient 8.0, peat albedo 0.235 — which is three numbers all saying the same
; thing, namely that the light was wrong rather than too low. Nine degrees of
; sun answered it properly and thirty-two answers it further: the same bog under
; **a 32-degree sun north-north-east and a sky full of cumulus**
; (`range_sky.tres`).
;
; The torches stay lit. They are a flame in a timber cresset and they cost
; nothing to keep, and a range the Elders use at any hour has torches on it at
; every hour. The twenty-four glowworm lanterns do not stay: they were unshaded
; spheres, and they are gone with the posts and with the whole idea of them
; (`range_map`, above `TORCHES`).
;
; Every number below was set against `preview_map` renders — `top`, `side` and
; the pads at eye height — read back by eye, in `rust_env.tres`'s tradition.
;
;   * **ALL ambient still comes from the sky** (`ambient_light_source = 3`,
;     contribution 1.0). **1.36, up from 1.15, and up from the dusk pass's 0.62
;     and a long way from the night's 8.0.** The 8.0 was a multiplier on a night
;     sky whose radiance cubemap averages near-black; 0.62 was that number made
;     honest under an apricot skyline; 1.15 is it made honest again under a blue
;     one, which carries less energy near the horizon than a sunset does and
;     therefore needs more of this knob, not less. The
;     ambient is no longer most of what the peat is lit by — that is the real
;     change of the hour. A key at 32 degrees lands sin(32) = 53.0% of itself on
;     a horizontal surface against sin(9)'s 15.6%, so the sun does the ground now
;     and the ambient does the shaded faces, which is the right way round and is
;     what makes a 1.25 m block read as a block.
;
;     **1.36 is D-169's, and it is the one knob that reaches a surface facing
;     *down*.** The key lights tops and north-north-east faces; `range.tscn`'s
;     `Clouds` fill lights everything facing up or south-south-west; the
;     underside of the lodge roof over the eight spawn pads faces neither, and
;     this dome — specifically its lower hemisphere, which `range_sky.tres`
;     lifted and cooled in the same pass — is all it has ever had. Measured in the
;     hall (`tools/range_views -- hall`, the frame the rack move is judged on):
;     the share of it at literal black went **6.6% to 0.0%** and its median 30 to
;     44 of 255, with the share blown out unchanged at 0.1%. The key came down
;     3.0 → 2.25 at the same time, so this is not a brighter picture with the
;     same shape in it: it is the same total light with less of it arriving warm
;     and from one direction.
;
;     `ambient_light_color` is (0.78, 0.8, 0.9) and **is not used**, because
;     `ambient_light_sky_contribution` is 1.0 and the sky is therefore 100% of
;     it. It is kept in step with the fill anyway — it is what this environment
;     would fall back to if anyone ever dialled the contribution off the dome,
;     and a stale value there is a trap laid for whoever does.
;   * **Volumetric fog 0.0025, down from the dusk pass's 0.0035 and the night's
;     0.010.** Thinner again for the reason it was thinned the first time: there
;     is more light in the air to pick it up, and a range you cannot see the far
;     end of is not a range. `volumetric_fog_anisotropy` is **0.3 rather than
;     0.6**, and that is the number that is purely about the hour — strongly
;     forward-scattering fog is what puts light *along* the ground under a sun
;     nine degrees up, and under one thirty-two degrees up it puts a hot patch
;     in the sky where the disc is and nothing on the peat. Near-isotropic is
;     what daylight haze actually does.
;   * **Height fog: 0.9 m at 0.015, down from 1.2 m at 0.10.** This is the
;     single biggest correction in the file and it was invisible at night. A
;     dense layer in the bottom metre under a cold moon is a hint of damp; the
;     same layer under a warm sky is a **lit** sheet, and a standing Bog's eyes
;     at 1.33 m look along sixty metres of it. The first sunset render had the
;     whole bog as a featureless pale plain with the cover blocks inside it, and
;     the tell was that the lodge deck at exactly 1.2 m — the old `fog_height` —
;     was the only clean surface in frame. `range_ambience.gd`'s own `FogVolume`
;     came down from 0.020 to 0.012 and went warm for the same reason.
;   * Distance fog **0.0014** with `fog_aerial_perspective` **0.30**, which is
;     the one thing a high sun wants *more* of: with no long shadows to give the
;     sixty-metre lane depth, the far end has to recede by colour instead.
;     `fog_sun_scatter` **0.08, down from 0.22** — scatter is most of the
;     picture with a 9-degree sun ahead-right of you and an artefact with one
;     32 degrees up, because the sun is no longer anywhere near what you are
;     looking at.
;   * **Glow threshold 2.8, not 2.4 and not 1.45.** 1.45 was above a moonlit Bog
;     and below a torch flame, and under a lit sky it is below the *sky*. 2.4
;     cleared a dusk cloud top; a white cumulus under a 32-degree sun is
;     brighter than a rose one under a 9-degree sun, so the threshold goes up
;     with it. It is still below the sun disc and the torch flames, which are now
;     the only two things on this map meant to bloom — the lantern heads that
;     were the third are gone. `glow_intensity` 0.7 with it.
;   * SSAO at **1.4**, down from 1.7 and the island's 2.2. On a map of flat slabs
;     it is most of what makes a 1.25 m block read as a block from twenty metres
;     when the sun is too low to cast a useful shadow. At 32 degrees a block
;     casts a 2.0 m shadow of its own, so the contact darkening can stand back
;     and let the real shadow do it. At 2.2 it was taking the unlit underside of
;     the lodge roof to literal zero over the eight spawn pads; see
;     `range.tscn`'s `Clouds` for the other half of that fix.
;   * `adjustment_saturation` back to 1.0 from 1.1. The night needed a push; a
;     lit sky through an ACES tonemap does not, and at 1.1 the peat went magenta
;     under the sunset and goes orange under this one.
;   * No SSR. Peat reflects nothing, and a screen-space pass over a map this long
;     is a cost with no image behind it.

[ext_resource type="Sky" path="res://resources/config/range_sky.tres" id="1_range_sky"]

